    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Without loss of generality, we may suppose that $B = \{  \beta : \beta < \beta _0 \} $ is a set of ordinals. We will define a sequence of filtered, $\tau $-small subsets $A(n) \subseteq A$ by induction on $n$. For $n = 0$, choose an element $\alpha \in A$ and set $A(0) = \{  \alpha \} $. Suppose next that $A(n)$ has been defined. We define a sequence of enlargements $\{  A(n)_{\beta } \} _{\beta \leq \beta _0}$ by induction on $\beta $. Let $A(n)_{0} = A(n)$, let $A(n)_{\lambda } = \bigcup _{\beta < \lambda } A(n)_{\beta }$ when $\lambda $ is a nonzero limit ordinal, and let $A(n)_{\beta +1}$ be a $\tau $-small, filtered subset of $A$ such that the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \colim _{A(n)_{\beta +1} } F_{\beta }(\alpha ) \rightarrow \colim _{A} F_{\beta }(\alpha ) \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a weak homotopy equivalence (such a subset exists in virtue of Lemma <a href="/tag/D1472B" data-tag="D1472B">5.4.3.1</a>). We now take $A(n+1) = A(n)_{\beta _0}$ and $A' = \bigcup _{n} A(n)$; it is easy to check that $A' \subseteq A$ has the desired properties. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
